statement:
Given a positive integer $n$, Marie plays a game where she starts with the number $1$ on a blackboard. As many times as she wants, she can choose an integer $j$ such that $1 \leq j \leq n$ and replace the number $V$ on the blackboard with the number $j \cdot R\!\left(\dfrac{V}{j}\right)$, where $R(x)$ denotes the nearest integer to $x$ (rounded up if $x$ is exactly halfway between two consecutive integers).
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that for each given $n$, there is a positive integer $B$ such that Marie can never end up with a number larger than $B$ on the blackboard.
\item[(b)] For any given $n$, let $f(n)$ be the maximum number obtainable on the blackboard after finitely many replacements. Show that there exists a positive integer $N$ such that for all $n \geq N$, $2026$ divides $f(n)$.
\end{enumerate}
